\title{VCV-io and Game-Based Cryptography in Lean}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The oracle framework [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) is built on: computations with oracle effects,
    handlers that implement them, and a program logic for the game-hopping arguments that security
    proofs are written as.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Oracle computations and \code{simulateQ}: implementing a random oracle, logging queries,
        performing reductions.}
        \li{The program logic — relational (pRHL-style) and unary modes — and what a game hop looks
        like as a tactic step.}
        \li{Primitives already covered: encryption, signatures, sigma protocols, and the
        [Fiat–Shamir](dhsorens-002G) and Fischlin transforms.}
        \li{How ArkLib's adversary model sits on top of this.}
        \li{What a machine-checked reduction proof costs in effort, compared with the paper proof.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [VCV-io](https://github.com/dtumad/VCV-io), [VCVio: Verified Cryptography in Lean via
    Oracle Effects and Handlers](https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/899).
}
